        "city": "Montreal",
        "market_heading": "Why virtual staging matters in the Montreal real estate market",
        "market_text": "<p>Montreal's housing stock is unlike anywhere else in Canada: duplexes and triplexes with tenant-occupied units, century-old walk-ups with unusual room shapes, and a fast-growing stock of new condos. Empty or cluttered, these spaces are hard for buyers to read — home staging shows what the crooked-charming rooms of a Plateau flat actually hold, capturing a buyer's attention and helping them understand the space from the first photo online.</p><p>Many plex listings are photographed mid-tenancy. Our furniture removal digitally clears the tenant's belongings first, then stages the space clean — no awkward photos, no waiting for a vacancy.</p><p>The plex market is the reason this matters so much here. A triplex buyer is underwriting three units they will mostly never see empty, and photos taken around a tenant's furniture make that impossible to assess. Cleared and staged, each unit can be judged on its own layout and light.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Virtual staging Montreal: plexes, condos and family homes",
        "local_text": "<p>Our designers specialize in furnishing to scale, and match each room to the neighbourhood and the buyer's lifestyle: European-leaning contemporary for Old Montreal and Griffintown, eclectic warmth for the Plateau and Mile End, family staging for NDG and the West Island. Images are ready for Centris, Realtor.ca and the MLS in 24 to 48 hours, with disclosure guidance, so your virtual staging services reach buyers the same week you list.</p><p>In Quebec, the OACIQ's advertising guideline says images that fundamentally alter a property's actual appearance violate the rules, and asks brokers to keep the original photos available on request. We add furniture and decor only — never structural changes — and our <a href=\"/pages/resources-mls-rules-canada\">Canadian virtual staging compliance guide</a> covers labelling on Centris and Realtor.ca.</p><p>Montreal's winters make the timing argument on their own — moving physical furniture into a second-floor walk-up in February is a job nobody wants, and the listing usually can't wait for spring. Volume discounts apply automatically: 5% at 3+ photos, 10% at 6+, 15% at 11+ and 20% at 21+.</p>",
        "hoods_label": "Where we stage most",
        "hoods": "Plateau Mont-Royal, Mile End, Griffintown, Old Montreal, Rosemont, Verdun, NDG, Outremont, West Island",
